WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.940
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, Episode 116, coming to you on June 29, 2026.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.380
Starting the week with the latest from space and science, supermassive black holes keep flinging

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.700
radio-emitting material into space years after swallowing a star, showing their appetite stay

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:23.320
active long after the initial feast. Very large array monitoring of dozens of stellar,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.980
tidal disruption events revealed radio outbursts long after the initial optical flare.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.520
The delayed emission indicates the black hole is ejecting portions of the disrupted star

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:40.800
at later times. These bursts allow astronomers to track changes in accretion state in real time.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:48.060
Very large array data show material is still being expelled long after the initial optical flare fades,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:54.980
indicating the accretion flow remains unstable. The timing of the flare lets astronomers watch

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:00.560
how the black hole's feeding rate changes over months to years. The next step is to monitor

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.320
a larger sample to learn whether every disruption produces delayed outflows or only a subset.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:13.140
If the sun were compressed to the size of a city, its mass would still fit inside a neutron star.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:19.840
A supermassive black hole at a galaxy center packs millions or billions of solar masses into a

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.000
region no larger than our solar system. When a star ventures too close, the black hole's gravity

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.180
stretches it into a stream of gas that falls inward and forms a temporary disk.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:38.320
Months or years later, some of that gas is flung outward again in narrow jets or winds, producing

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.560
bright radio emission as the particles spiral in magnetic fields. One measured delay reached

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.580
several years showing that the disk can store and later release material on time scales far

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:56.480
longer than the initial flare. We still do not know what suddenly changes inside the disk to trigger

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:02.480
the ejection after such a long pause. Now shifting to another case where early universe structures are

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.180
proving more mature than expected. James Webb's space telescope images captured a massive,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.460
densely packed galaxy cluster forming earlier than models predicted. The structure appears at a time

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:20.480
when the universe was roughly half its current age. Astronomers note the cluster's density and maturity

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.820
challenge current ideas about how quickly such systems assemble. Follow-up observations will target

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:32.980
member galaxies to measure star formation rates and dynamical state. Radar sounding of Europa

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:39.700
analogs on Earth has clarified how the Moon's ice shell may trap or exchange material with the

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:45.540
subsurface ocean. The work identifies layering and potential fracture zones that could connect the

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:51.580
surface to the ocean below. These features matter for future missions seeking biosignatures.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:58.300
NASA's Europa Clipper will carry its own ice penetrating radar to test the models. Researchers

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:05.100
mapped 18,000 star forming complexes in spiral galaxies to quantify how young stars inject

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:11.260
energy and momentum into their surroundings. The survey shows feedback strength varies with local

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:17.840
gas density and stellar population age. Results will feed into simulations that track how galaxies

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:24.000
regulate their own growth. Next steps include extending the analysis to more distant systems with

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.100
JWST, from early galaxy assembly to two planets that also formed under surprising conditions.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.240
Astronomers discovered a pair of extremely low density giant planets whose average densities

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:43.940
fall below that of candy floss. The worlds were weighed during Antarctic winter observations that

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:50.420
captured their mutual gravitational tugs. Both planets orbit the same host and perturb each other's

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:56.480
transit times. The team is now modeling how such diffuse envelopes can persist against stellar

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.780
irradiation. Observations show a giant planet altering the magnetic activity of its star through

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:09.040
tidal and magnetic interactions. The effect reverses the usual one-way relationship in which stars

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.780
dominate their planets. Data come from long-term monitoring of stellar chromosphoric emission

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:21.920
and planetary orbital parameters. Researchers plan continued spectroscopy to track how the interaction

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:29.460
evolves over multiple orbits. JWST transmission spectra of a warm inflated world revealed absorption

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:36.000
features consistent with sodium and potassium chloride clouds. The planet's unusual pink hue matches

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.820
laboratory spectra of these salts under the expected temperature and pressure conditions.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:47.120
The detection marks the first clear evidence of exotic cloud chemistry on a giant exoplanet.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:53.020
Follow-up observations will search for additional condensates and map cloud patchiness.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.380
From exotic planet formation to a more routine but commercially important satellite launch,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:07.880
SpaceX launched the SXM-11 satellite aboard a Falcon 9 from Cape Canaveral at 0225 UTC.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:15.840
The spacecraft, built by Maxar, will join the Sirius XM radio constellation. The first stage touched

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.640
down on the drone ship a shortfall of gravitas after separation. Deployment of the satellite was

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.540
confirmed shortly after reaching its transfer orbit. The mission forms part of an ongoing refresh

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:34.820
of the geostationary broadcast fleet. From operational satellite fleets to a startup restoring

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:42.240
its own commercial service. British startup Sattvoo declared HOT-SAT-2 operational on June 29,

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:47.840
restoring revenue generating services after its first satellite failed in 2023.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.820
The spacecraft delivers high-resolution thermal data for energy, industrial and environmental

00:05:53.820 --> 00:06:01.140
customers. Operations resumed from the company's existing ground segment. Sattvoo expects the satellite

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:07.140
to support multiple contracts already in the pipeline. Two strong quakes struck Venezuela last

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:14.040
week. The NASIR mission supplied radar imagery that shows how the ground moved during the events.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:20.520
NASA's disaster's response coordination system activated to distribute maps and data products

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:26.400
to emergency teams. The imagery helps responders assess damage and plan relief routes.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:32.800
Additional data releases are scheduled as processing continues. From one company's recovery to

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.740
a national effort scaling an even lower orbit regime, a new industry alliance will coordinate

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:44.760
development of satellites operating below 300 kilometers altitude. Several Chinese spacecraft

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.180
have already demonstrated sustained flight at these heights using continuous propulsion.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:55.160
The group also supports propulsion startup seeking investment for drag compensation systems.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:01.500
Sustained VLEO operations could enable higher resolution earth imaging and lower latency

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:08.000
communications. From new orbital regimes to quiet pockets of calm gas near the Galactic center.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:13.760
At a comma large millimeter sub millimeter array, observations uncovered a region of

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:20.040
slowed settled gas inside the turbulent Milky Way center. Within this pocket, material appears

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:26.240
to be collecting into dense cores that may become new stars. The finding suggests star formation

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.940
can proceed through familiar processes even in extreme environments. Further, a Taka

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:38.480
mallarge millimeter sub millimeter array maps will search for additional quiescent zones and measure

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:49.220
their magnetic and turbulent properties. The cluster may add SJ 0018.5 plus 1626 appears as a

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:56.260
single x-ray source but is actually two systems colliding head on. Radio and x-ray data reveal the

00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:02.620
ongoing merger and the resulting shock fronts. The alignment offers a rare view of cluster assembly

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:08.600
and progress. Additional multi wave length observations will track how the merger redistributes hot

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:15.940
gas and dark matter. Los Alamos researchers confirmed that a pair of long duration GRBs originated from

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:22.240
neutron stars collapsing into black holes rather than from typical collapse ours. The events were

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.920
identified through detailed modeling of their light curves and host galaxy properties. The finding

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:34.660
expands the known channels that produce long GRBs. Further gravitational wave searches will look

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:40.940
for similar progenitors. A new study explores how the sun's gravity could magnify light from compact

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:47.760
stellar remnants enabling resolved images of their surfaces or accretion discs. The technique would

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:55.180
require spacecraft positioned 550 AU or farther from the sun. The same alignment geometry already

00:08:55.180 --> 00:09:01.940
proposed for exoplanet imaging can be repurposed for these targets. Mission concepts are now evaluating

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:08.720
required pointing stability and integration times. Before we go keep an eye on continued very large

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:14.900
array follow up of tidal disruption events and the next round of James Webb Space Telescope cluster

